<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur I, Duke of Brittany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1187">1187</a>–c. 1203</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Arthur of Brittany · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">House of Plantagenet</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur of Brittany was the posthumous son of Geoffrey, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>, and Constance, duchess of Brittany. On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> in 1199 he claimed the Angevin inheritance against his uncle John, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>. Captured by John at Mirebeau in 1202, he disappeared in custody in 1203 and was widely believed to have been murdered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-i-duke-of-brittany.jpg" alt="Arthur I, Duke of Brittany" width="250" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artur_of_Brittany.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry II’s son Geoffrey ruled through his marriage to the heiress Constance, and after their son <b>Arthur of Brittany</b> disappeared in King John’s custody around 1203, Philip II of France gave the duchy to Peter Mauclerc of the house of Dreux in 1213.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After John’s marriage to Isabella of Angoulême, the court of Philip II of France declared his continental fiefs forfeit in 1202, and John’s suspected role in the death of his nephew <b>Arthur of Brittany</b> cost him the loyalty of many barons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Medieval councils set aside child heirs in favour of grown men: John was chosen King of England in 1199 because his nephew <b>Arthur of Brittany</b>, the heir by strict primogeniture, was a boy, and Parliament chose Henry IV in 1399 over the young Earl of March.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
